  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Question": "Lines \\(AB\\) and \\(CD\\) intersect at point \\(O\\). Which of the following pairs consists of vertical angles?",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(\\angle AOC\\) and \\(\\angle BOC\\)",
    "B": "\\(\\angle AOC\\) and \\(\\angle AOD\\)",
    "C": "\\(\\angle AOD\\) and \\(\\angle BOD\\)",
    "D": "\\(\\angle AOC\\) and \\(\\angle BOD\\)"
  },
  "Correct Answer": "D",
  "Accepted Answers": [],
  "Unordered Answers": false,
  "Solution": "Vertical angles require each side of one angle to be opposite a side of the other. Assume that \\(O\\) lies between \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), and between \\(C\\) and \\(D\\). Then \\(OA\\) and \\(OB\\) are opposite rays, as are \\(OC\\) and \\(OD\\), so \\(\\angle AOC\\) and \\(\\angle BOD\\) are vertical angles. Each of the other pairs has a common side and consists of adjacent supplementary angles. Under this assumption, the answer is D.\n\nHowever, the displayed statement does not specify that point order. For example, place \\(A,B\\) on opposite sides of \\(O\\), but place distinct points \\(C,D\\) on the same ray of another line through \\(O\\). The full lines still intersect at \\(O\\), yet every listed pair shares a ray, so none is a pair of vertical angles. Thus D is conditional, and the question as written does not determine a valid choice.",
